I added `recordLogin(email, callback)` and two tests. `npm test` passes: 12 of 12, including the two new tests. Lint is also clean.

**`src/services/auth-service.js`**
```js
export function recordLogin(email, callback) {
  findByEmail(email, (err, user) => {
    if (err) return callback(err);
    if (!user) return callback(null, null);
    update('users', user.id, { last_login_at: new Date().toISOString() }, callback);
  });
}
```
- It looks the user up with the existing `findByEmail`, which already skips soft-deleted users, so only an active user gets the timestamp.
- On success the callback gets the updated user row. If there's no active user with that email, it gets `(null, null)`, the same way `findByEmail` reports a missing user.
- The time is stored as an ISO string, the same format as `deleted_at`.

**`tests/auth-service.test.js`** has two new tests:
- An active user (`grace@example.com`) gets `last_login_at` set to the current time.
- A soft-deleted user (`alan@example.com`) is not updated, and the callback gets `null`.

`reset()` in `src/db.js` doesn't clear `last_login_at`, so Grace's timestamp stays in the in-memory data for the rest of a test run. No current test checks that field, so nothing is affected today.

